INSERT INTO orch_events(id, project_id, track_id, type, ts, payload) VALUES(4132, 'p-b5bd663dcf', 't-62504d3c56', 'worker_reported', '2026-09-28T14:56:56.120Z', '{"agent_id": "447b15e3-64ae-4a85-9a23-c8ebb29fad59", "role": "orchestrator", "model": "opencode-go/space-bunny-free", "task": "wake 15: GPU0 stale-100% util counter is a cross-track hazard; t0001 voided cleanly", "summary": "[#Orchestrator] NARROW POLL wake 15, 3 calls.\n\nCROSS-TRACK HAZARD, not restating t-d1cf43b723''s report but generalising it. Dev 6a24a038 reported that utilization.gpu reads a STUCK 100% on GPU0 with ZERO compute apps across 4 samples - a stale counter left by a SIGKILL, not live work. That is not local to that track: ANY reader of GPU util on that host will see a false 100% until it resets. t-a124729a8a is building Strata on GPU0 next, so a false \"busy\" read could block or corrupt that build. Any collector or leader trusting a GPU-busy signal on this host should verify against process count first, not util alone.\n\nAlso on t-d1cf43b723: owner dropped t0001 for t0003, leader v76 stopped arguing and executed. The dev voided the mid-step-1 artifact so a partial run can never read as a completed cell, retired the id, kept the 11777 MiB sm_120 point that falsified the capacity model by +1995 MiB / +20%, and reported STOPPED rather than root-causing. Good evidence hygiene, and it did not paper over a failed run.\n\nt-f3ba82ac9c still inside its 61-min cadence. t-a124729a8a healthy, new. Counter still 15/0, gen_started null, 15th wake.", "status": "partial", "pr": "", "artifacts": [], "reported_at": "2026-09-28T14:56:56.119Z"}');
INSERT INTO orch_events(id, project_id, track_id, type, ts, payload) VALUES(4133, 'p-b5bd663dcf', 't-62504d3c56', 'task_updated', '2026-09-28T15:00:20.608Z', '{"task_id": "task-23876ada06", "title": "B: store-layer paging correctness (filter-before-slice) + first Store tests", "changes": {"note": "REWRITTEN 2026-09-28 after verifying the live backend. The previous version of this task was wrong about which backend runs: it described the JSON backward-chunked scanner as the production path and cited its \"bounded memory\" as a reason to implement offset by over-read. Neither is true in production. Corrected body follows.\n\n=== CORRECTED TASK B ===\n\nPRODUCTION IS SQL. Verified against the live ledger, not assumed:\n  orchestration.sqlite mtime 2026-09-28T21:29Z, 7.8MB, 4116 events / 549 decisions / 2068 turns / 161 tasks / 10 tracks\n  systemd unit orchestration-backend.service sets Environment=MCP_ORCH_DB_PATH=.../orchestration.sqlite\n  The projects/<pid>/events.jsonl files on disk are STALE pre-cutover leftovers (newest 2026-09-27, one from 2026-09-02). Never read them to learn current state; grep the code or query the sqlite.\n  JSON mode is still reachable (empty MCP_ORCH_DB_PATH = JSON, db.py:203) and is load-bearing for tests + migration.py. Treat it as a parity/migration obligation, not a hot path.\n\nSCOPE 1 — push the filter into WHERE, and bound the fetch.\ntail_jsonl''s SQL branch (store.py:225-237) is:\n  SELECT ... FROM events WHERE project_id=? ORDER BY ts DESC, id DESC\n  ... for row in cur.fetchall():\nThere is NO LIMIT in the query. The limit is applied in a Python loop AFTER the whole project ledger is in memory, and the predicate is a Python callable that SQLite cannot evaluate — so filtering happens in Python, over a full fetch. That is true of production.\nConsequences, both real:\n (a) track_status makes FOUR tail_jsonl calls (reporting.py:501, 508, 527, 555). In SQL mode that is four full-ledger fetches per call to build a 10-line summary, three of them over 4116 events. This is the actual cost driver on a long track.\n (b) The predicate-as-callable design is the structural blocker: SQL cannot push a Python closure down. A minimal fix (accept optional structured kwargs alongside the predicate) leaves that debt in place. A real fix introduces a small filter dataclass that each backend compiles — JSON to a closure, SQL to a WHERE clause. Prefer the real fix; the whole point of B is that production does the filtering.\n\nSCOPE 2 — offset paging must not break the filter. Reproduced against live data:\n  filter type=''task_updated'' (199 of 2827 events on p-7719544617), page offset=30 limit=5\n    slice-then-filter (LIMIT/OFFSET then filter)  -> 0 rows\n    filter-then-slice (WHERE type=? then LIMIT/OFFSET) -> 5 rows\nA naive SQL OFFSET returns an EMPTY page and silently skips 5 records. The predicate must be in WHERE, and the existing indexes (idx_events_project_ts, idx_events_track_ts, idx_events_type) make it cheap. Make this exact case a test.\n\nSCOPE 3 — keep the (ts, id) tiebreaker. It is load-bearing, not defensive. Measured on the live ledger:\n  - 121 same-project row groups share an IDENTICAL ts\n  - 1 adjacent-id ts inversion (wall clock went backwards once)\nSo ORDER BY ts DESC, id DESC is correct and must stay. ORDER BY id DESC alone would be wrong on that inversion. The JSON branch (stable sort on ts, no tiebreaker) is the INCORRECT side — fix JSON to match SQL, do not \"reconcile\" them the other way.\n\nSCOPE 4 — turns are the asymmetric case. Turns page by n, covered by idx_turns_track_n ON turns(track_id, n). There is NO (track_id, ts) index — idx_turns_project_ts is (project_id, ts). So you cannot write an efficient WHERE ts >= ? for one track. Resolve the time cutoff to an n FIRST (legitimate: n is monotonic per track via next_turn_number, store.py:896), then page on WHERE n >= ?. That resolution must happen before LIMIT/OFFSET. Do not add an index for this; do not filter turns by ts in SQL.\n\nVERIFIED PRECONDITION — ts is safe for range predicates. All 4130 events and 2073 turns are exactly 24 chars, zero non-canonical. utcnow_iso() is fixed-width, so lexicographic compare == chronological AND the index is usable. Do not start parsing/normalising ts. Note: age_seconds also tolerates \"+00:00\" and naive strings (models.py), but the ledger contains none — if a future writer emits one, SQL range queries silently misorder. Consider a CHECK constraint as a follow-up, not in this task.\n\nACCEPTED TRADEOFF, a comment not a TODO: no keyset cursor. OFFSET walks and discards; fine at 4116 events, revisit past ~100k. Do not build an index speculatively beyond SCOPE 2.\n\nTESTS. Still no direct Store test (no test_store.py); tail_jsonl / stream_jsonl_filtered / read_turns_paged are covered only transitively. Create tests/test_store.py. Prioritise the SQL branch — that is production:\n  - the SCOPE 2 case (filter + offset, asserts 5 not 0)\n  - ts/id tie ordering with a synthetic same-ms group AND a synthetic inversion\n  - offset past end -> []; offset 0 first page\n  - the ts -> n cutoff resolution on turns\n  - a dual-mode parity test (test_sql_backend.py:191 asserts res_j == res_s)\n  - JSON-only, lower priority: >64 KiB file to exercise the chunk-boundary leftover path (store.py:303-341), empty file, corrupt line mid-file. Note migration.py:55-58 silently SKIPS corrupt lines while store.py:322 raises StateError — the two disagree today; pin whichever you keep.\n  - non-numeric turn filename is silently dropped by `p.stem.isdigit()` (store.py:532) — pin that or fix it, but say which.\n\nGATES. test_sql_backend.py green in both modes. Full suite green."}, "by": "leader"}');
