INSERT INTO orch_events(id, project_id, track_id, type, ts, payload) VALUES(4229, 'p-b5bd663dcf', 't-a124729a8a', 'task_updated', '2026-09-28T18:11:36.782Z', '{"task_id": "task-17049c781b", "title": "S4 (dsh Phase 4, 14cf3334be): load-older affordance — interactive row, not just a scroll threshold (dsh shipped the scroll-only version first and it was not enough)", "changes": {"status": "done", "progress": 100, "note": "ACCEPTED 2026-09-29, commit d1bd4602b, 5 files (+524/-90), plugin-only, worktree clean, 159/159 on an independent run. Leader-verified: the S4 diff contains only CONTEXT lines for geometry - no height constant, no getItemLayout entry, no windowing prop moved, as briefed; the control is testID=\"load-older\" at :487 with hasOlderHistory (:116) hiding it and a disabled-while-loading state (:118). The reverse read is real: loadOlder at :213 over a ref (:60), olderInFlight separate from the drain''s inFlight (:74/:161/:165/:193), list({agentId, beforeSeq: oldest}) at :169, hasOlderHistory state at :50/:62. Putting the control ABOVE the list rather than inside it was the right design: no new row type means no new height to calibrate, and it stays reachable while the user watches the tail. hasOlderHistory derived from \"a page shorter than the limit is the end of history\" is a good inversion - absence of data is the signal. FIVE TH LEADER BRIEF ERRORS NOW, and this one is worse than the previous four because it was a wrong NEGATIVE presented as a finding: I reported onStartReached as unconfirmed on native RN 0.81.5 and it is declared at @react-native/virtualized-lists@0.81.5/Lists/VirtualizedList.d.ts:314 - my grep returned nothing because VirtualizedList was extracted out of react-native into its own package and react-native/Libraries/Lists/VirtualizedList.js is a 25-line re-export shim, so a Libraries/-scoped grep can never find a prop declared in the extracted package. The dev also found maintainVisibleContentPosition exists on native but has NO .d.ts declaration even there, so depending on it would not have typechecked; the imperative scrollToOffset path was the only one that works on both runtimes. The dev explicitly declined to claim the visual result is verified: the tests pin the ARITHMETIC of anchor preservation, the pixels need the browser, which is S3e''s. That distinction is recorded and is not to be elided. Two self-corrections by the dev are recorded: its own assertion that one press should cover [1,1200] was wrong (one press is one page of 500, [201,700]), rewritten to assert the real contract which now also proves the cursor advances; and its anchor test initially failed because it prepended before pressing, when the real order is press then fetch then prepend then restore."}, "by": "leader"}');
