INSERT INTO orch_events(id, project_id, track_id, type, ts, payload) VALUES(6492, 'p-b5bd663dcf', 't-a124729a8a', 'task_updated', '2026-10-03T17:05:53.958Z', '{"task_id": "task-17a27083bc", "title": "G1: the trajectory plugin is ungated at three levels — not a workspace member, no CI, and the pre-commit typecheck cannot see it; monorepo typecheck is red at base (225 undeclared-dep errors)", "changes": {"status": "pending", "progress": 0, "assignee": "owner", "note": "2026-10-03 DO NOT DISPATCH THIS TASK ON THE GATE. The final-wake digest (collector generation 44) named task-17a27083bc as \"dispatchable with no owner input: gate ready, 0%\" and said it is the one thing a new leader can start immediately. That is wrong, and acting on it reproduces the defect this task exists to record. THIS GATE IS IMMUTABLY WRONG. It reads `ready` while its own detail says the five candidate fixes are the owner''s to choose, with the explicit instruction \"do not pick unilaterally - (c) and (d) are shared/repo-level\". I attempted to set user-decision and the server REFUSED: gate, like status, is immutable after creation, so the field tooling reads cannot be corrected in place. This note is the override, and it is now the third time I have had to restate it. WHY DISPATCHING WOULD BE HARMFUL, concretely: (a) make plugins/ workspace members - touches root package.json, a repo-level file affecting every lane; (b) scope the lefthook typecheck job the way format and lint already are, or baseline-diff against the base commit''s error set - also repo-level, and it changes a hook every agent in every lane depends on; (c) declare the undeclared deps in packages/app, packages/website, packages/desktop - cross-lane shared files requiring an announcement per d-12a0c8b77a, and may not even resolve if the packages are genuinely absent rather than merely undeclared; (d) fix the monorepo typecheck to green at 225 pre-existing errors - a substantial piece of work on its own. Three of the five candidates are repo-level or cross-lane, and a unilateral pick would either fork shared state or commit someone to a large project nobody authorised. ALSO NOTE THE DIGEST''S OWN COUNTS ARE INCONSISTENT: its table lists four pending tasks (this one, task-d115d5618d, task-24a2ae9fa4, task-c90d30c62b) and one done, but its summary line says \"2 done, 3 open\". Minor, but it is the same class of count I have caught repeatedly this session, so I am not treating any aggregate in that digest as authoritative without re-deriving it. WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY UNBLOCK THIS, cheaply and with no repo-level risk: the plugin-scoped gates already exist and are green - plugin tests, plugin tsc --noEmit, format:check and lint scoped to the changed files. The smallest change that restores signal without pretending the repo is green is (b)-with-a-baseline-diff, or simply documenting in the repo that plugins/ is not a workspace member, which also closes the T0 item \"plugins/ is not a workspace member - document or integrate into repo test setup\". Both are owner calls, not leader calls. Correct assignee is owner; correct gate is user-decision. Recorded here so a fresh leader reading the gate field alone does not dispatch on it."}, "by": "owner"}');
