PRODUCTION IS SQL. Verified against the live ledger, not assumed:
  orchestration.sqlite mtime 2026-09-28T21:29Z, 7.8MB — 4116 events / 549 decisions / 2068 turns / 161 tasks / 10 tracks
  systemd unit orchestration-backend.service sets Environment=MCP_ORCH_DB_PATH=.../orchestration.sqlite
  The projects/<pid>/events.jsonl files on disk are STALE pre-cutover leftovers (newest 2026-09-27, one from 2026-09-02). Never read them for current state — grep the code or query the sqlite.
  JSON mode is still reachable (empty MCP_ORCH_DB_PATH = JSON, db.py:203) and is load-bearing for tests + migration.py. Treat it as a parity/migration obligation, NOT a hot path.
The superseded task called the JSON backward-chunked scanner the production path and cited its bounded memory as the reason to implement offset by over-read. Neither is true in production.

SCOPE 1 — push the filter into WHERE, and bound the fetch.
tail_jsonl''s SQL branch (store.py:225-237) is:
  SELECT ... FROM events WHERE project_id=? ORDER BY ts DESC, id DESC
  ... for row in cur.fetchall():
No LIMIT in the query. The limit is applied in a Python loop AFTER the whole project ledger is in memory, and the predicate is a Python callable SQLite cannot evaluate — so filtering happens in Python over a full fetch. This is production behaviour.
 (a) track_status makes FOUR tail_jsonl calls (reporting.py:501, 508, 527, 555). In SQL mode that is four full-ledger fetches per call to build a 10-line summary, three of them over 4116 events. That is the real cost driver on a long track.
 (b) The predicate-as-callable design is the structural blocker — SQL cannot push a Python closure down. A minimal fix (extra structured kwargs beside the predicate) leaves that debt in place. A real fix introduces a small filter dataclass each backend compiles: JSON to a closure, SQL to a WHERE clause. Prefer the real fix; production doing the filtering is the whole point of this task.

SCOPE 2 — offset paging must not break the filter. Reproduced against live data:
  filter type=''task_updated'' (199 of 2827 events on p-7719544617), page offset=30 limit=5
    slice-then-filter (LIMIT/OFFSET then filter)   -> 0 rows
    filter-then-slice (WHERE type=? then LIMIT/OFFSET) -> 5 rows
A naive SQL OFFSET returns an EMPTY page and silently skips 5 records. The predicate must be in WHERE; idx_events_project_ts / idx_events_track_ts / idx_events_type make it cheap. Make this exact case a test.

SCOPE 3 — keep the (ts, id) tiebreaker. It is load-bearing, not defensive. Measured on the live ledger: 121 same-project row groups share an IDENTICAL ts, and there is 1 adjacent-id ts inversion (wall clock went backwards once). So ORDER BY ts DESC, id DESC is correct and must stay; ORDER BY id DESC alone would be wrong on that inversion. The JSON branch (stable sort on ts, no tiebreaker) is the INCORRECT side — fix JSON to match SQL, never the reverse.

SCOPE 4 — turns are the asymmetric case. Turns page by n, covered by idx_turns_track_n ON turns(track_id, n). There is NO (track_id, ts) index; idx_turns_project_ts is (project_id, ts). So an efficient WHERE ts >= ? for a single track is not available. Resolve the time cutoff to an n FIRST (sound: n is monotonic per track via next_turn_number, store.py:896), then page on WHERE n >= ?. That resolution must precede LIMIT/OFFSET. Do not add an index for this, and do not filter turns by ts in SQL.

VERIFIED PRECONDITION — ts is safe for range predicates. All 4130 events and 2073 turns are exactly 24 chars, zero non-canonical, because utcnow_iso() is fixed-width. So lexicographic compare == chronological AND the index is usable. Do not start parsing or normalising ts. Caveat worth a follow-up: age_seconds also tolerates "+00:00" and naive strings (models.py) but the ledger contains none; a future writer emitting one would make SQL range queries silently misorder. A CHECK constraint is out of scope here.

ACCEPTED TRADEOFF, a comment not a TODO: no keyset cursor. OFFSET walks and discards; fine at 4116 events, revisit past ~100k.

TESTS. Still no direct Store test (no test_store.py) — tail_jsonl / stream_jsonl_filtered / read_turns_paged are covered only transitively through the tool layer. Create tests/test_store.py, prioritising the SQL branch because that is production:
  - the SCOPE 2 case (filter + offset; asserts 5 rows, not 0)
  - ts/id tie ordering with a synthetic same-ms group AND a synthetic inversion
  - offset 0 first page; offset past end -> []
  - the ts -> n cutoff resolution on turns
  - dual-mode parity (test_sql_backend.py:191 asserts res_j == res_s)
  - JSON-only, lower priority: >64 KiB file to exercise the chunk-boundary leftover path (store.py:303-341); empty file; corrupt line mid-file. Note migration.py:55-58 silently SKIPS corrupt lines while store.py:322 raises StateError — the two disagree today, so pin whichever you keep.
  - non-numeric turn filename is silently dropped by `p.stem.isdigit()` (store.py:532) — pin or fix, but say which.

