REPRODUCED on the live ledger, not theorised. I posted a 5341-char correction to task-23876ada06 via task_update(note=...). Where it landed:
  events ledger, task_updated payload.changes.note -> 5341 chars, retrievable
  tasks table                                                    -> NO note column
  tasks table columns: id, track_id, project_id, title, detail, gate, status, progress, assignee, added_at, updated_at
`track_status.tasks` is the ONLY way an agent reads a task, and it does not carry `note`. So the correction was invisible exactly where it was needed, while the stale, wrong body stayed authoritative in `detail`. Worse, task_update has no `detail` parameter at all — so there is NO tool path to amend a task''s own spec. It is write-once by construction.

This is not cosmetic. A task body is an instruction to a worker. If the body is wrong and cannot be edited, the only options are cancel-and-recreate (which loses the task id, the assignee, the progress, and the mirror history) or leave a known-wrong instruction in place and hope the reader notices. On a fleet that dispatches work from these bodies, that is a correctness hole, not an ergonomics complaint.

SCOPE.
1. Add `note: str = ""` to QueueItem (state/models.py) and wire it through task_update so a note is PERSISTED, not just evented. This is the same gap task C documents from the other direction.
2. Add a `detail` (or `amend`) capability to task_update so a task body can be revised in place, preserving id, assignee and progress. An amendment should be an append-only trail, not a silent overwrite — the ledger can record the prior body the way override_recorded records provenance, so "what was this task supposed to say at dispatch time" stays answerable.
3. Surface both in track_status so a worker reading the queue sees the current body AND any amendments.

MIGRATION COST — this is the expensive part, and it is why it is its own task rather than folded into C. Adding a QueueItem field touches five sites: the tasks DDL (state/db.py:63-75), save_track''s DELETE+re-INSERT (store.py:717-720), get_track''s row->dict build (store.py:771), list_tracks (store.py:801), and the field projection in scripts/verify_sqlite_parity.py:190-202. Existing rows default to empty, so this is additive — but the parity script will FAIL until its projection is updated, and that failure is the point: it is the check that the two backends still agree.

SEQUENCING. Depends on nothing; blocks nothing. But it is a prerequisite for safely correcting ANY future task body, so do it before the fleet starts dispatching from amended specs. If the five-site migration is judged too expensive right now, the acceptable interim is: delete and re-add the task with corrected text, and say so in the turn_report — not to leave a wrong body in place.

