f6a7f797f meter | cf261120b claude | 5ad8fea6c opencode + registry wiring.

ENFORCEMENT IS NOW REAL for the two harnesses that can be enforced:
• opencode — injects provider.<id>.models.<m>.limit.context through the ALREADY-WIRED decorateServerEnv seam (no new plumbing). Only models seen in the runtime catalog are capped, so no phantom provider.models entry is invented.
• claude — extraArgs.autocompact with the 100k floor.
• meter — displayMax = min(runtimeReported, cap), cap read via useDaemonConfig + config.providers[agent.provider].maxContextTokens (the T7 precedent). Meter already saturated, so no meter change was needed.

ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH, as instructed: resolveProviderContextCap() is now the single reader of the configured value, used by BOTH the registry mapper (what Paseo advertises) and the provider clients (what the harness enforces), threaded via ProviderClientFactoryOptions.maxContextTokens → AgentSessionConfig.maxContextTokens → claude assertConfig. No second resolver.

THE NON-OBVIOUS PROBLEM I SOLVED: caps are written into config at SPAWN, but the model list is only learned from the catalog AFTERWARD — so the obvious implementation silently leaves caps inert until something else restarts OpenCode. Each server generation now records a cap fingerprint and the agent requests a fresh generation when the live one lacks the desired caps. Covered by a test asserting a "new" acquisition, plus the reuse case.

GATES: opencode+registry+cap 220/220; claude options 11/11; app context-window 11/11; lint 0/0 on 14 files; format:check repo-wide CLEAN (4808 files); typecheck 19768 = baseline EXACTLY. I verified zero-new properly: stripped line numbers and diffed the entire error multiset, since my inserted tests shifted lines and a naive comm produced 43 phantom "new" errors. The only real diff is one pre-existing error whose message differs by property order inside its type printout.

MY MISTAKES, OWNED: (1) I added getCurrentContextCapsKey to OpenCodeServerManagerLike and did not update TestOpenCodeHarness — +67 TS errors at once, caught by measuring rather than assuming. (2) used `it` in a file importing only describe/expect/test. (3) omitted required providerOptions on OpenCodeAgentConfig in 3 new tests. (4) 3 oxlint no-useless-fallback-in-spread in my own context-cap.ts.

TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN DESIGN NOTE, caught by subagents inspecting the binary: (a) claude''s [100k,1M] range is enforced by REJECTION — the argParser throws — so the floor is load-bearing, not cosmetic: without the clamp a 50k cap would CRASH the agent at startup, and a >1M cap also throws, so it emits nothing rather than clamping down. (b) the SDK forwards extraArgs keys verbatim, not kebab-cased.

ENV DEFECTS blocking naive gate commands (pre-existing, both proved by stash): the claude vitest scope includes *.real.e2e.test.ts that need OPENROUTER_API_KEY and spawn real agents — use --exclude "**/*.e2e.test.ts" (repo convention); with it 30 files/410 tests pass. 6 packages/app composer suites fail to COLLECT on unbuilt @getpaseo/plugin — needs npm run build:client.

DEFERRED per your instruction, untouched: acp affordance, omp --config probe. Note §4/§8 unchanged, but §3''s "floored at 100k" is superseded by (a).

NOT RUNTIME-VERIFIED — the honest gap: I never drove a live agent to watch compaction actually fire. Everything above is source- and decompile-derived plus unit tests. QC r28 must prove the effect, on opencode, since claude cannot express a sub-100k cap.', 'idle', '[]', '[]', '[]', '[]', '{"lesson_topic": null, "docs_updated": []}', '236a054c-6d31-403c-8909-0d2d91da4864', 'opencode-go/space-bunny-free', '{"n": 325, "ts": "2026-10-03T05:25:16.047Z", "summary": "PHASE B COMPLETE — 3 commits on feat/t12-ctx-enforcement, 19 files +1008/-18. No PR, no deploy (leader batches after QC r28).\n\nf6a7f797f meter | cf261120b claude | 5ad8fea6c opencode + registry wiring.\n\nENFORCEMENT IS NOW REAL for the two harnesses that can be enforced:\n• opencode — injects provider.<id>.models.<m>.limit.context through the ALREADY-WIRED decorateServerEnv seam (no new plumbing). Only models seen in the runtime catalog are capped, so no phantom provider.models entry is invented.\n• claude — extraArgs.autocompact with the 100k floor.\n• meter — displayMax = min(runtimeReported, cap), cap read via useDaemonConfig + config.providers[agent.provider].maxContextTokens (the T7 precedent). Meter already saturated, so no meter change was needed.\n\nONE SOURCE OF TRUTH, as instructed: resolveProviderContextCap() is now the single reader of the configured value, used by BOTH the registry mapper (what Paseo advertises) and the provider clients (what the harness enforces), threaded via ProviderClientFactoryOptions.maxContextTokens → AgentSessionConfig.maxContextTokens → claude assertConfig. No second resolver.\n\nTHE NON-OBVIOUS PROBLEM I SOLVED: caps are written into config at SPAWN, but the model list is only learned from the catalog AFTERWARD — so the obvious implementation silently leaves caps inert until something else restarts OpenCode. Each server generation now records a cap fingerprint and the agent requests a fresh generation when the live one lacks the desired caps. Covered by a test asserti